1
00:00:05,970 --> 00:00:13,160
An architect builds a model and draws it from three directions: from the front, from the side, and from above.

2
00:00:13,210 --> 00:00:16,280
The view from above is called the plan.

3
00:00:16,330 --> 00:00:18,210
Why does she need all three?

4
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:22,810
Could two different buildings look the same from the front?

5
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:26,400
Could two different solids have the same front view?

6
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:29,660
Pause the video and decide.

7
00:00:29,710 --> 00:00:29,940
Yes.

8
00:00:29,990 --> 00:00:34,720
A cylinder and a cuboid can both look like a rectangle from the front.

9
00:00:34,770 --> 00:00:38,000
You need other views to tell them apart.

10
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:43,030
Remember: a triangular prism has 5 faces, 9 edges and 6 vertices.

11
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:48,140
A square-based pyramid has 5 faces, 8 edges and 5 vertices.

12
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:54,030
For any solid with flat faces and no holes, faces plus vertices minus edges equals 2.

13
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:56,030
This is Euler's rule.

14
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:56,690
Your turn.

15
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:59,080
What is F plus V minus E for a cube?

16
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:02,600
Pause the video and work it out.

17
00:01:02,650 --> 00:01:06,620
A cube has 6 faces, 8 vertices and 12 edges.

18
00:01:06,670 --> 00:01:09,280
6 plus 8 minus 12 is 2.

19
00:01:09,330 --> 00:01:12,050
The plan is the view from directly above.

20
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:19,150
The front elevation is the view from the front, and the side elevation is the view from the side.

21
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:24,510
Here is a solid made of 4 cubes: 3 in a row, with 1 on top of the left cube.

22
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:28,380
From the front it looks like an L of 4 squares.

23
00:01:28,430 --> 00:01:32,360
From the side, you see 2 squares, one on top of the other.

24
00:01:32,410 --> 00:01:35,590
From above, you see 3 squares in a row.

25
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:36,870
Here is the method.

26
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:43,310
Step 1: name the shape from its cross-section, for a prism, or its base, for a pyramid.

27
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:47,170
Step 2: count the faces, vertices and edges carefully.

28
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:49,440
Check with Euler's rule.

29
00:01:49,490 --> 00:01:54,140
Step 3: draw the plan, front and side elevations to scale.

30
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:58,280
Step 4: use the views together to identify a solid.

31
00:01:58,330 --> 00:02:00,050
A pyramid has 7 vertices.

32
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:03,020
One is the top, so the base has 6 corners.

33
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:05,410
It is a hexagonal pyramid.

34
00:02:05,460 --> 00:02:08,770
It has 7 faces: the base and 6 triangles.

35
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:13,180
It has 12 edges: 6 round the base and 6 up to the top.

36
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:16,190
Check: 7 plus 7 minus 12 is 2.

37
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:17,600
The plan is a circle.

38
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:20,550
The front and side views are both triangles.

39
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:21,690
What solid is it?

40
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:25,120
It is round from above, and pointed from the sides.

41
00:02:25,170 --> 00:02:26,540
It is a cone.

42
00:02:26,590 --> 00:02:27,220
Your turn.

43
00:02:27,270 --> 00:02:30,160
What is the plan of a cylinder standing up?

44
00:02:30,210 --> 00:02:32,720
Pause and choose.

45
00:02:32,770 --> 00:02:36,890
From directly above, a standing cylinder looks like a circle.

46
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:41,190
From the front and the side, it looks like a rectangle.

47
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:43,650
A shape has 6 faces and 12 edges.

48
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:46,110
Kim finds the number of vertices.

49
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:48,750
Which line goes wrong first?

50
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:49,890
Line 1 is wrong.

51
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:52,890
Euler's rule is F plus V minus E equals 2.

52
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:55,870
You cannot have negative vertices!

53
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:59,270
6 plus V minus 12 equals 2, so V equals 8.

54
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:01,360
It could be a cube.

55
00:03:01,410 --> 00:03:08,040
A classic football is made of 12 pentagons and 20 hexagons, so it has 32 faces.

56
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:11,290
It has 60 vertices and 90 edges.

57
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:15,120
Check Euler's rule: 32 plus 60 minus 90 is 2.

58
00:03:15,170 --> 00:03:18,130
It works even for a football.

59
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:19,400
One last question.

60
00:03:19,450 --> 00:03:20,890
A prism has 15 edges.

61
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:23,090
How many vertices does it have?

62
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:26,410
Pause the video and answer.

63
00:03:26,460 --> 00:03:30,970
A prism with an n-sided end has 3 n edges and 2 n vertices.

64
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:34,220
15 edges means n is 5, a pentagonal prism.

65
00:03:34,270 --> 00:03:36,470
So it has 10 vertices.

66
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:41,630
To remember: for solids with flat faces, F plus V minus E equals 2.

67
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:43,790
The plan is the view from above.

68
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:48,200
The elevations are the views from the front and the side.

69
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:50,580
Draw the views to scale.

70
00:03:50,630 --> 00:03:54,390
Use several views together to identify a solid.

71
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:59,470
Great work! Now practise lesson 8.3 on Camday.
